            <div style="padding-left: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 15px;">Ways to recover after exercise:</h3>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Hydrate - Make sure to drink plenty of water after each workout. You will want to replace as much sweat that you lost and then some.</li>
                    <li>Water is your body's principal chemical component and makes up about 50% to 70% of your body weight. Your body depends on water to survive. Every cell, tissue and organ in your body needs water to work properly. For example, water:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li>Gets rid of wastes through urination, perspiration and bowel movements</li>
                            <li>Keeps your temperature normal</li>
                            <li>Lubricates and cushions joints</li>
                            <li>Protects sensitive tissues</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li>Lack of water can lead to dehydration — a condition that occurs when you don't have enough water in your body to carry out normal functions. Even mild dehydration can drain your energy and make you tired.</li>
                    <li>Sleep - Sleep is when your body will be actively recovering from your workout, rebuilding your muscles and other tissues.</li>
                    <li>Eat - Your body needs fuel in order to rebuild those tissues. Try eating a protein-rich meal after each workout, especially if you have been strength training.</li>
                    <li>Stretch - Light stretching can help get the blood moving more into your joints and bring it to hard-to-reach places. Five to ten minutes of stretching after a workout will lessen the likelihood of feeling as sore the next day as well.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
